d memory on a wireless chip), not about system-level access control or privilege management.\n\nThis clearly falls under Class 0 (Not High)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 7778986299, "prompt_eval_count": 380, "eval_count": 607}}, {"id": "security_compute_av", "ground_truth": "1", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in vulnerability severity evaluation based on CVSS v3.1 standards.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Not High)**: These functions are general system utilities, hardware/driver interactions, and basic resource management operations (e.g., reading/writing registers, memory mapping, configuring hardware) that do not alter security policies or require administrative privileges. Common keywords include \"read\", \"write\", \"map\", \"unregister\", \"validate\", \"check\", and basic \"get/set\" operations for hardware states. They lack logic patterns related to access control or privilege management.\n\n**Class 1 (High)**: These functions involve security enforcement, access control management, capability checks, and system-wide privileged configurations (e.g., modifying security IDs, checking mount/socket permissions, managing keyrings). Common keywords include \"privilege\", \"capable\", \"security\", \"permission\", \"acl\", \"sid\", \"policy\", \"relabel\", and names of security modules like \"tomoyo\" or \"apparmor\". Their logic patterns center around authorizing, altering, or bypassing system-level access controls and administrative rules.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Not High, (B) High\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I will give you a function co