butes to security identifiers - this is access control management\n- Is part of security enforcement mechanism (SELinux networking)\n\nThis function is clearly about security enforcement and access control management - specifically converting NetLabel security attributes to SELinux security identifiers. This is core SELinux functionality for managing security contexts for network labels.\n\nThe classification should be Class 1 (High).\n\nWait, let me re-read the classification options:\n(A) Not High, (B) High\n\nSo my answer should be (B) High."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 10562657261, "prompt_eval_count": 471, "eval_count": 776}}, {"id": "sk_ns_capable", "ground_truth": "1", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in vulnerability severity evaluation based on CVSS v3.1 standards.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Not High)**: These functions are general system utilities, hardware/driver interactions, and basic resource management operations (e.g., reading/writing registers, memory mapping, configuring hardware) that do not alter security policies or require administrative privileges. Common keywords include \"read\", \"write\", \"map\", \"unregister\", \"validate\", \"check\", and basic \"get/set\" operations for hardware states. They lack logic patterns related to access control or privilege management.\n\n**Class 1 (High)**: These functions involve security enforcement, access control management, capability checks, and system-wide privileged configurations (e.g., modifying security IDs, checking mount/socket permissions, managing keyrings). Common keywords include \"privilege\", \"capable\", \"security\", \"permission\", \"acl\", \"sid\", \"policy\", \"relabel\", and names of security modules like \"tomoyo\" or \"apparmor\". Their logic patterns c