tem-wide privileged configurations (e.g., modifying security IDs, checking mount/socket permissions, managing keyrings). Common keywords include \"privilege\", \"capable\", \"security\", \"permission\", \"acl\", \"sid\", \"policy\", \"relabel\", and names of security modules like \"tomoyo\" or \"apparmor\". Their logic patterns center around authorizing, altering, or bypassing system-level access controls and administrative rules.\n\nCLASSIFICATION OPTIONS:\n(A) Not High, (B) High\n\nRemember: You must heavily penalize the classification unless there is CLEAR evidence. When in doubt, prefer the safer option.\n\nLet's think step-by-step to reach the right conclusion."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I will give you a function context. Classify it based on the expert knowledge provided.\n\nFunction: smk_tskacc\nFunction description: smk tskacc determine if task ha specific access to an object this function check the subject task is label object label pair in the access rule list and return if the access is permitted non zero otherwise it allows that the task may have the capability to override the rule\n\nBased on the expert knowledge provided, classify the function. After reasoning, output your final answer EXACTLY in this format:\n**Answer: (X) Label**"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "stream": false, "think": true}, "response": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "created_at": "2026-04-07T19:50:18.290639877Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Looking at this function carefully:\n\n**Function**: `smk_tskacc`\n\n**Analysis**:\n1. The \"smk\" prefix strongly indicates this is part of **Smack** (Simple Mandatory Access Control Kernel), a well-known Linux security module.\n2. The function **determines if a task has specific access to an object** \u2014 this is core access control logic (checking subject label vs. object label against an access rule list).\n3. It explicitly mentions that the task **\"may have the capability to override the rule\"** \u2014 this involves capability/privilege checking.\n4. The function returns whether access is **permitted or not** \u2014 this is a security enforcement/authorization decision.\n\nAll these characteristics align directly with Class 1 (High):\n- It involves **security enforcement** and **access control management**\n- It involves **capability checks** (allowing privilege overrides)\n- It deals with **authorizing** system-level access control decisions\n- \"smk\" relates to a security module (Smack), similar to keywords like \"tomoyo\" or \"apparmor\" mentioned in the Class 1 definition\n\nThis is not a general utility or hardware operation \u2014 it is a security policy enforcement function.\n\n**Answer: (B) High**", "thinking": "Let me analyze this function carefully.\n\nFunction: smk_tskacc\nDescription: \"smk tskacc determine if task ha specific access to an object this function check the subject task is label object label pair in the access rule list and return if the access is permitted non zero otherwise it allows that the task may have the capability to override the rule\"\n\nBreaking down the description:\n1. It determines if a task has specific access to an object\n2. It checks the subject task's label and object label pair in the access rule list\n3. It returns whether access is permitted (non-zero) or not\n4. It allows that the task may have the capability to override the rule\n\nKey indicators:\n- \"smk\" prefix - this is likely from Smack (Simple Mandatory Access Control Kernel), a Linux security module\n- \"tskacc\" - task access, clearly an access control function\n- It checks subject/object label pairs against access rule lists - this is MAC (Mandatory Access Control) logic\n- It mentions \"capability to override the rule\" - this involves privilege/capability checking\n\nThis is clearly a security enforcement function related to access control. It's part of the Smack security module which is a Linux security module for mandatory access control.\n\nAccording to the classification rules:\n- Class 1 (High) involves \"security enforcement, access control management, capability checks\"\n- Keywords include \"privilege\", \"capable\", \"security\", \"permission\", \"acl\", \"sid\", \"policy\"\n- Logic patterns center around \"authorizing, altering, or bypassing system-level access controls\"\n\nThis function clearly falls into Class 1 (High) because:\n1. It's an access control decision function (checking if access is permitted)\n2. It involves capability checks (allowing capability override)\n3. It's part of a security module (Smack)\n4. It deals with subject/object label pairs for access decisions\n\nThe answer is (B) High."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 79143918106, "prompt_eval_count": 420, "eval_count": 662}}, {"id": "gasket_ioctl_check_permissions", "ground_truth": "1", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in vulnerability severity evaluation based on CVSS v3.1 standards.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Not High)**: These functions are general system utilities, hardware/driver interactions, and basic resource management operations (e.g., reading/writing registers, memory mapping, configuring hardware) that do not alter security policies or require administrative privileges. Common keywords include \"read\", \"write\", \"map\", \"unregister\", \"validate\", \"check\", and basic \"get/set\" operations for hardware states. They lack logic patterns related to access control or privilege management.\n\n**Class 1 (High)**: These functions involve security enforcement, access control management, capability checks, and system-wide privileged configurations (e.g., modifying security IDs, checking mount/socket permissions, managing keyrings). Common keywords include \"privilege\", \"capable\", \"security\", \"permission\