: This function makes retry decisions on an ELS (Extended Link Service) command IOCB that has failed. It handles retrying ELS commands like FLOGI, PLOGI, PRLI, ADISC, LOGO, and FDISC based on the command type and returned error status. It decides whether to retry immediately or delay the retry.\n\nLet me check against the classification criteria:\n\n**Class 0 (Not High)**: General system utilities, hardware/driver interactions, basic resource management operations (reading/writing registers, memory mapping, configuring hardware). Keywords: \"read\", \"write\", \"map\", \"unregister\", \"validate\", \"check\", basic \"get/set\" operations for hardware states. Lacks logic patterns related to access control or privilege management.\n\n**Class 1 (High)**: Security enforcement, access control management, capability checks, system-wide privileged configurations (modifying security IDs, checking mount/socket permissions, managing keyrings). Keywords: \"privilege\", \"capable\", \"security\", \"permission\", \"acl\", \"sid\", \"policy\", \"relabel\", security modules like \"tomoyo\" or \"apparmor\". Logic patterns center around authorizing, altering, or bypassing system-level access controls and administrative rules.\n\nLooking at lpfc_els_retry:\n- This is a function related to a Fibre Channel driver (lpfc is a Linux Fibre Channel driver)\n- ELS stands for Extended Link Service - these are Fibre Channel protocol operations\n- FLOGI (Fabric Login), PLOGI (Port Login), PRLI (Process Login), ADISC (Address Discovery), LOGO (Logout), FDISC (Fabric Discovery) are all Fibre Channel protocol operations\n- This function handles retry logic for failed ELS commands\n- It's a driver-level function dealing with hardware protocol communication\n- It doesn't involve security enforcement, access control management, capability checks, or system-wide privileged configurations\n- The keywords and logic don't match Class 1 criteria\n\nThis function is clearly a hardware/driver interaction fu