ivilege management.\n\n**Class 1 (High)**: Functions involving security enforcement, access control management, capability checks, system-wide privileged configurations. Keywords like \"privilege\", \"capable\", \"security\", \"permission\", \"acl\", \"sid\", \"policy\", \"relabel\", and names of security modules like \"tomoyo\" or \"apparmor\". Logic patterns around authorizing, altering, or bypassing system-level access controls.\n\nAnalysis:\n- This is a test function for cgroup (control group) internal process constraints\n- Cgroups are about resource management, not specifically security enforcement or access control\n- The function tests whether you can add a pid to a domain parent when a controller is enabled - this is about cgroup hierarchy/process management constraints\n- This doesn't involve security enforcement, access control, capability checks, or privilege management\n- It's testing internal cgroup logic about process constraints\n- \"Constraint\" here refers to a cgroup mechanism for controlling which processes can be moved between cgroups, not a security/access control mechanism in the CVSS sense\n\nThis function is more about testing cgroup internal behavior (resource management) rather than security enforcement or access control. It's a test function that validates cgroup behavior, not a security-critical function.\n\nTherefore, this should be classified as Class 0 (Not High)."}, "done": true, "done_reason": "stop", "total_duration": 12646810084, "prompt_eval_count": 390, "eval_count": 756}}, {"id": "mei_hbm_fw_add_cl_req", "ground_truth": "0", "prompt": {"model": "glm-5.1:cloud", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are Frederick, an elite AI cybersecurity expert specializing in vulnerability severity evaluation based on CVSS v3.1 standards.\n\nTo ensure extreme accuracy, you MUST strictly follow this domain knowledge when making your decision:\n\n**Class 0 (Not High)**: These functions are general system utilities, hardware/driver int